Q: Must blockquotes contain paragraphs or must paragraphs contain blockquotes? A: <p>According to <a href="http://www.w3.org/TR/html5/grouping-content.html#the-blockquote-element" rel="noreferrer">the specifications provided by the W3 group</a> the <code>blockquote</code> is a block level semantic that should contain the entire quote, which can be made up of multiple paragraphs. Because the <code>blockquote</code> itself contains one or more paragraphs(or other elements), it doesn't make much sense to put it inside a paragraph. An example, provided by the W3G:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;blockquote&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;My favorite book is &lt;cite class="from-source"&gt;At Swim-Two-Birds&lt;/cite&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;footer&gt;- &lt;cite&gt;Mike[tm]Smith&lt;/cite&gt;&lt;/footer&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>As you can see, the example <code>blockquote</code> contains text that's inside of <code>p</code> elements, but not inside of <code>q</code> elements.</p>

<p><a href="http://www.w3.org/TR/html5/text-level-semantics.html#the-q-element" rel="noreferrer">The <code>q</code> element is a text level semantic</a> and it should be used to indicate that part of the text is a quotation. Because the <code>blockquote</code> already indicates quotation, marking the text inside with <code>q</code>-elements is not necessary. An example, taken from the W3G specification:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;p&gt;The man said &lt;q&gt;Things that are impossible just take longer&lt;/q&gt;. I disagreed with him.&lt;/p&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>Long story short: if you use a <code>blockquote</code> element, put other elements (such as <code>p</code> elements) inside of it, but it's not necessary to use <code>q</code> elements inside a <code>blockquote</code>.</p>

Q: What is the difference between CSS fit-content and max-content? A: <p>It seems these two codes are the same:</p>
<pre><code>.fit-content {
  width: fit-content;
}
// is same as
.fit-content {
  width: max-content;
  max-width: 100%;
  min-width: min-content;
}
</code></pre>
<p>In my experience I either go with <code>width: fit-content</code> or <code>width: max-content; max-width: 100%</code>. The latter is for cases when the element shouldn't have a min-width.</p>

Q: Multiple function calls, but single execution of async Task A: <p>I encountered a similar challenge recently - maybe you’ll find the solution I came up with useful (it’s more of syntactic sugar, to be honest)</p>
<p>Created a blog post about it if you are interested in details: <a href="https://buczel.com/blog/swift-task-coalescing/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Swift Task Coalescing: From Duplicate Callers to a Single Request</a></p>
<p>Code:</p>
<pre><code>/// An actor that coalesces concurrent async tasks to prevent duplicate operations.
///
/// When multiple callers request the same operation concurrently:
/// 1. Executes the operation only once for a given key
/// 2. Shares the result with all callers awaiting that key
/// 3. Automatically cleans up completed tasks
public actor InFlightTaskCoalescer&lt;Key: Hashable &amp; Sendable, Value: Sendable&gt; {
    private var inFlightTasks: [Key: Task&lt;Value, Error&gt;] = [:]

    public init() {}

    /// Executes an operation, deduplicating concurrent calls with the same key.
    ///
    /// If an operation with the given key is already in flight, this method awaits
    /// and returns its result instead of starting a new operation.
    public func deduplicated(
        key: Key,
        priority: TaskPriority? = nil,
        operation: @escaping @Sendable () async throws -&gt; Value
    ) async throws -&gt; Value {
        if let existingTask = inFlightTasks[key] {
            return try await existingTask.value
        }

        let task = Task(priority: priority) {
            try await operation()
        }

        inFlightTasks[key] = task

        defer {
            inFlightTasks[key] = nil
        }

        return try await task.value
    }

    /// Cancels the in-flight task for the given key, if one exists.
    @discardableResult
    public func cancel(key: Key) -&gt; Bool {
        guard let task = inFlightTasks[key] else {
            return false
        }

        task.cancel()
        inFlightTasks[key] = nil
        return true
    }

    /// Cancels all in-flight tasks.
    public func cancelAll() {
        for task in inFlightTasks.values {
            task.cancel()
        }
        inFlightTasks.removeAll()
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Angular - How to set multiple mat-footer in mat table A: <p>Here is a 'quick and dirty' example - the main thing to notice is that the col names for the extra footers can't be the same as used for the normal ones - then it will just use those multiple times. (Edited your functions just to show that it returns different values, as the current data returned the same values :) )</p>
<p><a href="https://stackblitz.com/edit/angular-material-table-with-multiple-footer-czgvn9?file=src/app/app.component.ts" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://stackblitz.com/edit/angular-material-table-with-multiple-footer-czgvn9?file=src/app/app.component.ts</a></p>
<p>Here's the template code:</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;table mat-table [dataSource]=&quot;transactions&quot; matSort class=&quot;table table-hover&quot;&gt;
  &lt;ng-container
    *ngFor=&quot;let columnName of displayedColumns&quot;
    [matColumnDef]=&quot;columnName&quot;
  &gt;
    &lt;th mat-header-cell *matHeaderCellDef mat-sort-header class=&quot;text-danger&quot;&gt;
      {{ columnName }}
    &lt;/th&gt;
    &lt;td mat-cell *matCellDef=&quot;let row&quot;&gt;{{ row[columnName] }}&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;td mat-footer-cell *matFooterCellDef=&quot;let row&quot;&gt;
      {{columnName === 'item' ? 'Min': getMinValue(columnName)}}
    &lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;td mat-footer-cell *matFooterCellDef=&quot;let row&quot;&gt;
      {{columnName === 'item' ? 'Min_ID': getMinItem(columnName)}}
    &lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;td mat-footer-cell *matFooterCellDef=&quot;let row&quot;&gt;
      {{columnName === 'item' ? 'Min below 10': countMinItem(columnName)}}
    &lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;/ng-container&gt;

  &lt;ng-container
    *ngFor=&quot;let columnName of displayedColumns2&quot;
    [matColumnDef]=&quot;columnName&quot;
  &gt;
    &lt;td mat-footer-cell *matFooterCellDef=&quot;let row; let i = index&quot;&gt;
      {{columnName === 'f2_item' ? 'Min_ID': getMinItem(columnName)}}
    &lt;/td&gt;
   &lt;/ng-container&gt;

    &lt;ng-container
    *ngFor=&quot;let columnName of displayedColumns3&quot;
    [matColumnDef]=&quot;columnName&quot;
  &gt;
    &lt;td mat-footer-cell *matFooterCellDef=&quot;let row; let i = index&quot;&gt;
      {{columnName === 'f3_item' ? 'Min below 10': countMinItem(columnName)}}
    &lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;/ng-container&gt;


  &lt;tr mat-header-row *matHeaderRowDef=&quot;displayedColumns&quot;&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;tr
    mat-row
    *matRowDef=&quot;let row; columns: displayedColumns&quot;
    (click)=&quot;getRecord(row)&quot;
  &gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;tr mat-footer-row *matFooterRowDef=&quot;displayedColumns&quot;&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;tr mat-footer-row *matFooterRowDef=&quot;displayedColumns2&quot;&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;tr mat-footer-row *matFooterRowDef=&quot;displayedColumns3&quot;&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
</code></pre>
